    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "628ff5505c6b090b6fdff149",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5f2e0a4e8464150716ffd322",
        "alias": "Aegean blue Kangaroo",
        "aliasID": "65c26da3ed6eefce4c4d0953",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/136933d7e6819e9dd6fd40cacf201a65.png",
        "created": "2020-08-08T02:13:34.907Z",
        "name": "ZenithVal",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2022-05-26T21:46:56.886Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "611eda5ff9f6600a64e5132e",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This is a bad idea.\n\"Collider Checks, and Dynamic Transforms  are much heavier of factors for global performance.\"\nThis statement is very misinformed.\nThere's MANY more factors behind the poly count being where it is besides the number. EG: a high poly 32 bit mesh when combined with things like massive amounts of shapekeys IS a huge performance hog. I do agree the automatic very poor at 70k is bad but this isn't how you fix this problem.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "6292449614dfd40b77cb6238",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5c0b6d3fbb15270c48b56338",
        "alias": "Probable Porcupine",
        "aliasID": "65c2c10ced6eefce4c7ea9ef",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/e8d506d18493dbdff10eaafac85d0fe8.png",
        "created": "2018-12-08T07:05:35.059Z",
        "name": "Xan",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2022-05-28T15:49:42.912Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "611eda5ff9f6600a64e5132e",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 1
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "A lotta bit of a word wall, but I'm clearing up a common misconception. Bear with me here.\nWhen you tell people \"polygons don't affect performance as much as ____\", you're forgetting the big asterisk that comes after that sentence. Polycount performance goes hand in hand with the performance of bones, shape keys, vertex shaders, and much more. To put it in a crude analogy, polygons are like a \"combo multiplier\" for the performance impact of other objects. By themselves, they can be pretty cheap to compute, but in practice, they should NEVER be considered cheap out of the gates. It is widely dependent on the model itself. It's why a static, unlit mesh with 10 million polygons will perform rather well, but if that same exact mesh were lit and had rigging, animating it would perform so terribly that it makes powerpoint look next gen. Same polycount, polar opposites performance-wise.\nThe 70k barrier is more of a way of the SDK saying \"it's reasonable to assume that at this point your model needs work, regardless of what is actually going on\", rather than it being your typical gradient of performance like with other parts of your avatar. It's a necessary evil that is not made very clear to people unless they have this knowledge ahead of time, and it doesn't help that folks go around saying \"polycount doesn't matter\". A model with, say, 50k *could* be fine, but it could also be terrible. Again, it depends on what your model is doing.\nChanging the value has the same exact problem. If it were bumped up to 100k, what benefit would it have? Would it be any more (or less) accurate? Those questions are flat out impossible to answer definitively. \"Yes\" and \"No\" are perfectly valid answers at the same time, because for the third time, *it depends.*",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-08-19T22:25:35.715Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "100k + should be Very poor... this doesn't make sense that it goes straight from good at 32k to Very Poor above 70k Tris..... Can we add some grey area here for medium and poor optimization Levels.... need to make incentive for creators to at least try and optimize..... 70K is too strict of a limit IMO that highly discourages creators from even attempting... Even though this is one of the least performant impacting factor of  avatars..... \nCollider Checks, and Dynamic Transforms  are much heavier of factors for global performance.",
